@title Building Permit Routing
@subtitle How the Whitcombe City planning office routes a permit application
@author Whitcombe City Planning Office
@date Current as of 2026-10-01

# Routing flow
diagram route dir=TB title="Permit application routing"
route: recv "Application received" user
route: complete "Submission complete?" decision
route: ret "Return to applicant" user
route: value "Project value over $50,000?" decision
route: fast "Fast-track review" svc
route: hist "In a historic district?" decision
route: heritage "Heritage Board review" team
route: plan "Plan review" team
route: issued "Permit issued" svc focus
route: recv -> complete
route: complete -> ret : no
route: complete -> value : yes
route: value -> fast : no
route: value -> hist : yes
route: hist -> heritage : yes
route: hist -> plan : no
route: heritage -> plan : cleared
route: fast -> issued : approved
route: plan -> issued : approved

# Review times
table = Stage,Target; Completeness check,within 2 business days; Fast-track review,within 3 business days; Plan review,within 15 business days; Heritage Board review,"within 30 days, on top of plan review"

! Heritage Board review is added to plan review, not instead of it. A project in a historic district waits up to 30 days for the Heritage Board and then up to 15 business days for plan review.

# Fees
kpi Base fee | $180 | every application | neutral
kpi Value fee | 0.8% | of project value above $50,000 | neutral

Example: a $150,000 project pays $180 plus 0.8% of $100,000 ($800), so $980 in total. A project of $50,000 or less pays only the $180 base fee.
